options:
  parameters:
    author: Matthieu
    catch_exceptions: 'True'
    category: '[GRC Hier Blocks]'
    cmake_opt: ''
    comment: "A modulated link you can watch - Fishball7020\n\nTHIS FLOWGRAPH TRANSMITS. Read\
      \ this part before you run it.\n\nThe board reaches roughly +19 dBm at an antenna port,\
      \ through a power\namplifier. What leaves that port is your responsibility and nobody\
      \ else's:\ntransmitting without a licence is illegal in most of the spectrum, and this\n\
      radio tunes 70 MHz to 6 GHz, which is nearly all of it. The default centre is\n2437\
      \ MHz, inside the 2.4 GHz ISM band, because that is the least bad default -\nnot because\
      \ it is automatically permitted where you are. Check your own\nregulations, and prefer\
      \ a cable and an attenuator to an antenna.\n\n  * `arm` starts UNTICKED and nothing\
      \ is transmitted until you tick it.\n  * `TX1 attenuation` starts at 89.75 dB, the most\
      \ the AD9361 offers.\n    HIGHER IS QUIETER. The safe end of that slider is the right-hand\
      \ end.\n  * The attenuation shown beside `arm` is not an echo of the slider. It is\n\
      \    read back out of the chip by an IIO Attribute Source, four times a\n    second.\
      \ If the driver refuses a write - the thermal limit of patch 0018,\n    the transmit\
      \ latch of patch 0016 - the slider will move and that number\n    will not. Believe\
      \ the number.\n  * A LOOPBACK WITHOUT AN ATTENUATOR DESTROYS THE RECEIVER. The RX input\
      \ is\n    rated about +2.5 dBm (AD9361 Rev. G, Table 11) and this board transmits\n\
      \    about +19. Fit at least 20 dB of pad. See docs/rf-safety notes and\n    tools/tx-guard.sh.\n\
      \nWHAT IT DOES. Random bytes become QPSK (or 16-/64-QAM), get shaped by a root\nraised\
      \ cosine, transmitted, received, matched-filtered, timing-recovered,\ncarrier-recovered,\
      \ and measured. The constellation, the eye and a live EVM\nfigure all come from the\
      \ same recovered symbols.\n\nTWO EVM NUMBERS, and the gap between them is the interesting\
      \ one. The first is\nthe error as it arrives. The second is the error after dividing\
      \ out a single\ncomplex gain - one fixed amplitude and one fixed rotation, fitted across\
      \ the\nblock, which is what a real vector analyser does before quoting a figure. The\n\
      difference is the share of your error that is a static rotation you could have\ncalibrated\
      \ away rather than noise you could not. Mistune the carrier loop and\nwatch the two\
      \ separate.\n\nTHE CAVEAT THAT MATTERS. This board is listening to itself, so the\n\
      transmitter and the receiver share one reference clock. There is no frequency\noffset\
      \ to track and no independent phase noise, and the EVM is therefore\nbetter than the\
      \ same modulation would achieve between two radios. It is a real\nmeasurement of a link\
      \ that is easier than any real link.\ndocs/modulation-gallery.md measured this path\
      \ with a separate radio and is the\nhonest comparison.\n\nOFFSET TUNING, AT BOTH ENDS.\
      \ The transmitter's own carrier leak sits at its\nLO, and the receiver's at its own.\
      \ Both offsets default to a fraction of the\nspan so that neither leak lands on the\
      \ signal. Set them both to 0 and watch\ntwo spikes appear in the middle of your own\
      \ transmission."
    copyright: ''
    description: QPSK/QAM over the air, with constellation, eye and live EVM
    gen_cmake: 'On'
    gen_linking: dynamic
    generate_options: qt_gui
    hier_block_src_path: '.:'
    id: fishball_modulated_link
    max_nouts: '0'
    output_language: python
    placement: (0,0)
    qt_qss_theme: ''
    realtime_scheduling: ''
    run: 'True'
    run_command: '{python} -u {filename}'
    run_options: prompt
    sizing_mode: fixed
    thread_safe_setters: ''
    title: Fishball7020 - a modulated link you can watch  (TRANSMITS)
    window_size: (1800,1100)
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 8
    - 8
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
blocks:
- name: import_math
  id: import
  parameters:
    imports: import math
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 8
    - 180
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: qam
  id: epy_module
  parameters:
    source_code: "\"\"\"Square QAM points, shared by the modulator and the EVM meter.\n\n\
      This is a GRC \"Python Module\" block, not a signal-processing block. Its\ncontents\
      \ are available in parameter expressions anywhere in the flowgraph, so\n`qam.points(order)`\
      \ can feed the constellation object that the MODULATOR is\nbuilt from, while the same\
      \ function decides what the EVM METER measures\nagainst. One definition, both ends.\n\
      \nThat matters more than it looks. An EVM meter holding its own idea of the\nconstellation\
      \ is a meter that can read a healthy few percent while the\ntransmitter is sending something\
      \ else entirely - the numbers stay plausible\nbecause both sides are internally consistent\
      \ and wrong together. Sharing the\nfunction makes that failure impossible rather than\
      \ unlikely.\n\n`order` is 4 (QPSK), 16 or 64. Points come out with unit mean power.\n\
      \"\"\"\n\nimport numpy as np\n\n\ndef bits(order):\n    \"\"\"Bits per symbol. 2 for\
      \ QPSK, 4 for 16-QAM, 6 for 64-QAM.\"\"\"\n    b = int(round(np.log2(order)))\n    if\
      \ 2 ** b != int(order):\n        raise ValueError('order must be a power of two')\n\
      \    return b\n\n\ndef points(order):\n    \"\"\"Unit-mean-power square QAM points,\
      \ as a list of Python complex.\n\n    Kept identical to examples/lib/evm_meter.py's\
      \ square_qam(), which\n    examples/test_blocks.py asserts.\n    \"\"\"\n    m = int(round(np.sqrt(order)))\n\
      \    if m * m != int(order) or m < 2:\n        raise ValueError('order must be a square:\
      \ 4, 16, 64 ...')\n    lv = 2 * np.arange(m) - (m - 1)                  # -3 -1 1 3\
      \ for m = 4\n    pts = (lv[:, None] + 1j * lv[None, :]).ravel()\n    pts = pts / np.sqrt((np.abs(pts)\
      \ ** 2).mean())\n    return [complex(p) for p in pts]\n"
    comment: 'Square QAM points. Feeds BOTH the constellation the modulator

      is built from and the reference the EVM meter measures

      against, so the two cannot drift apart.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 8
    - 260
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: uri
  id: variable
  parameters:
    value: '''ip:fishball.local'''
    comment: '`python3 tools/board_addr.py` prints an address if mDNS is not

      working on your network.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 176
    - 12
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: samp_rate
  id: variable
  parameters:
    value: '2000000'
    comment: '2 MS/s each way, so 500 ksym/s at 4 samples per symbol. Modest on

      purpose: 8 MB/s in each direction, and with the buffer below it

      gives the transmitter 524 ms of slack against a link stall -

      twice what patch 0015''s mute watchdog needs. Raise it once you

      have watched the thing work, and read the buffer note first:

      transmitting is latency-critical in a way receiving is not.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 256
    - 12
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: sps
  id: variable
  parameters:
    value: '4'
    comment: 'Samples per symbol, so 1 Msym/s at the default rate. Fixed when

      the modulator is built.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 336
    - 12
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: order
  id: variable
  parameters:
    value: '4'
    comment: '4 = QPSK, and QPSK is what this receiver actually recovers.

      16 and 64 will transmit correctly and will NOT resolve at the

      receiver - which is worth seeing once, and is explained in the

      note. A re-run parameter either way: the modulator takes its

      constellation object at construction, and the number of bits

      packed per symbol is fixed with it.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 416
    - 12
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: tx_alpha
  id: variable
  parameters:
    value: '0.35'
    comment: 'The TRANSMIT root-raised-cosine roll-off. Baked into the

      modulator''s taps, so changing it needs a re-run. The receive

      side has its own, live, control - see the note.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 496
    - 12
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: buf
  id: variable
  parameters:
    value: '1048576'
    comment: 'libiio buffer, in samples, both directions. At 2 MS/s this is

      524 ms of samples, and the transmit buffer''s DURATION is how long

      a link stall the DAC can ride out. Patch 0015 mutes the

      transmitter after 250 ms of starvation, so this is twice the

      margin that needs. Disarming is still immediate - the attenuator

      is analogue and unbuffered, so it does not wait for the buffer to

      drain. See the note.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 576
    - 12
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: center_hz
  id: variable
  parameters:
    value: int(center_mhz * 1e6)
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 664
    - 12
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: cnst
  id: variable
  parameters:
    value: qam.points(order)
    comment: 'Just the points; the Constellation Object block turns them into

      the object the modulator needs.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 744
    - 12
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: cnst_obj
  id: variable_constellation
  parameters:
    type: calcdist
    sym_map: list(range(order))
    const_points: qam.points(order)
    rot_sym: '4'
    dims: '1'
    normalization: digital.constellation.POWER_NORMALIZATION
    precision: '8'
    soft_dec_lut: None
    comment: 'Built from qam.points(order), the same function the EVM

      meter uses. Power normalisation makes the transmit level

      independent of the order.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 176
    - 560
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
