- name: note_level
  id: note
  parameters:
    note: "Why the transmit scale defaults to 0.20 and not 1.0.\n\nThe modulator produces\
      \ symbols with an RMS of 1.0 - and PEAKS well above it,\nbecause a root raised cosine\
      \ overshoots between symbols. Measured here, over\n400k samples:\n\n    QPSK    roll-off\
      \ 0.35   peak 1.57   PAPR 3.9 dB\n    16-QAM  roll-off 0.35   peak 2.03   PAPR 6.2 dB\n\
      \    64-QAM  roll-off 0.20   peak 2.37   PAPR 7.5 dB\n\ngr-iio's sink takes +/-1.0 as\
      \ the converter's full scale, so feeding it the\nmodulator's output unscaled clips the\
      \ peaks by up to 7.5 dB. Clipping is\nbroadband: it puts your signal where you did not\
      \ put it, which is a licensing\nproblem as well as a quality one.\n\n0.20 keeps the\
      \ worst case at 0.474, about -6.5 dBFS, which is the same\nheadroom tools/selftest/sdr_selftest.py\
      \ transmits at. The ceiling on the\nslider is 0.45 for the same reason. If you raise\
      \ it, watch the spectrum: the\nshoulders coming up is clipping, not power."
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 176
    - 300
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_buffer
  id: note
  parameters:
    note: "Why the buffer is big, and what a stream of U means.\n\nReceiving tolerates a slow\
      \ link: samples pile up on the board and you lose\nsome, which prints O for overflow.\
      \ TRANSMITTING DOES NOT. The converter has to\nbe fed in real time, so if a buffer arrives\
      \ late the DAC runs dry and prints U\nfor underflow - and on this firmware that is worse\
      \ than untidy. Patch 0015\nmutes the transmitter after 250 ms of starvation and switches\
      \ the data source\nto the internal DDS, so a link hiccup takes your signal off the air\
      \ and leaves\nthe flowgraph looking like it is still working.\n\nThat was measured here,\
      \ not imagined. Driving transmit and receive together at\n4 MS/s over a wireless host\
      \ link produced bursts of 20 to 40 underflows in 45\nseconds, each next to a 'Unable\
      \ to push buffer: Connection timed out' - while\na transmit-only stream at the same\
      \ rate and buffer produced none. It is\nintermittent: the same configuration ran clean\
      \ later the same hour, which is\nwhat you would expect of contention on a shared radio\
      \ channel rather than a\nthroughput limit.\n\nThe defence is buffer DURATION - buf /\
      \ sample_rate - because that is the length\nof stall you can absorb:\n\n    buf = 1048576\
      \     slack      vs the 250 ms watchdog\n    at 4 MS/s          262 ms    about equal\
      \ to it\n    at 2 MS/s          524 ms    twice\n    at 1 MS/s         1050 ms    four\
      \ times - the default here\n\nwhich is why this example is modest about the rate. Lowering\
      \ it buys slack AND\nlowers the traffic, so it helps twice; raising the buffer only\
      \ helps once. A\nwired link makes all of this go away, and\ndocs/modulation-and-throughput.md\
      \ has the throughput side of the same story.\n\nONE MORE TRAP, if you stop and immediately\
      \ restart this flowgraph:\n\n    RuntimeError: Unable to create buffer: -16\n\n-16 is\
      \ EBUSY. The board holds the transmit DMA for a moment after a client\ndisconnects,\
      \ so a new transmit buffer requested straight away is refused. It is\nnothing to do\
      \ with the buffer's SIZE - a 1048576-sample buffer allocates fine\nas the first request\
      \ of a fresh process, and a 262144-sample one is refused as\nthe second request of the\
      \ same process. Wait two seconds and start it again."
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 176
    - 380
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_qam
  id: note
  parameters:
    note: "Set `order` to 16 and watch the constellation fail. That is real.\n\nThe transmitter\
      \ is fine at any order - it is a table of points and a filter.\nThe RECEIVER is a QPSK\
      \ receiver, and a QPSK receiver does not become a QAM\nreceiver by changing one number.\
      \ This one recovers QPSK at about 0.65% EVM at\n40 dB SNR, measured; at 16-QAM the same\
      \ chain reads about 21% and the cloud\nnever resolves into sixteen points.\n\nWHY, concretely.\
      \ Both recovery loops here lean on the constellation being\neffectively constant-modulus:\n\
      \n  * The timing detector is decision-directed Mueller and Mueller, which is\n    built\
      \ around two-level decisions. On a multilevel constellation its error\n    signal is\
      \ dominated by which AMPLITUDE a symbol had rather than by the\n    timing error, so\
      \ the loop is being driven by the data.\n  * A Costas loop of order 4 recovers a carrier\
      \ from four-fold symmetry. A\n    16-QAM constellation has that symmetry, but its phase\
      \ error estimate is\n    also amplitude-dependent, so the same problem appears again.\n\
      \nGardner's detector, which needs no decisions, does not rescue it: measured on\nthis\
      \ chain it did not lock at ALL - a magnitude spread of 0.30 against 0.002\nfor Mueller\
      \ and Mueller, and about 45% EVM on a noiseless signal. A\ndecision-directed LMS equaliser\
      \ after the carrier loop made 16-QAM worse, not\nbetter (21% to 35%).\n\nWhat a QAM\
      \ receiver actually needs is joint decision-directed timing and\ncarrier recovery against\
      \ the full constellation - in GNU Radio, something built\naround `constellation_receiver_cb`,\
      \ or an equaliser that is properly trained\nrather than blind. That is a bigger piece\
      \ of work than a showcase, and it is\nthe honest reason this example stops at QPSK rather\
      \ than pretending.\n\nIf you want to SEE the higher orders transmitted properly, look\
      \ at the\nspectrum and the eye, which are receiver-independent. Both are correct at\
      \ any\norder."
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 176
    - 460
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_rerun
  id: note
  parameters:
    note: 'Which knobs are live, and which need a re-run.


      LIVE, because the block exposes a setter: attenuation, centre frequency,

      receive gain, both loop bandwidths, the RX matched filter''s roll-off, both LO

      offsets, arm, transmit scale.


      RE-RUN, because the value is fixed when a block is constructed: `order`

      (the modulator is built from its constellation), `sps`, `tx_alpha` (the

      transmit filter''s taps are baked into the modulator) and the buffer sizes.


      That is why there are TWO roll-off controls. `tx_alpha` is what the

      transmitter shapes with and it cannot change while running. `rrc_alpha` is

      the receiver''s matched filter, which can. Leave them equal and the filter is

      matched; drag `rrc_alpha` away from 0.35 and watch EVM climb. A matched

      filter is only matched if somebody keeps it that way.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 176
    - 540
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: bits
  id: analog_random_source_x
  parameters:
    type: byte
    min: '0'
    max: '255'
    num_samps: '100000'
    repeat: 'True'
    comment: 'Random bytes. Nothing here is a real protocol - the point

      is the modulation, not the payload.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 176
    - 660
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: mod
  id: digital_constellation_modulator
  parameters:
    constellation: cnst_obj
    differential: 'False'
    samples_per_symbol: sps
    excess_bw: tx_alpha
    verbose: 'False'
    log: 'False'
    truncate: 'False'
    comment: 'Bits to shaped symbols. differential=False: the carrier

      loop''s 90-degree ambiguity would scramble the bits, but

      nothing here decodes bits, and EVM does not care.'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
